            <ArticleTitle>[Hypnosis as an alternative treatment for pain in palliative medicine].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>674-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10354-008-0620-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pain--which can have a variety of causes--constitutes a severe problem for patients in need of palliative care, because this pain usually dramatically impairs their quality of life. Thus, the more advanced a terminal illness has become, the more hospital staff should focus on holistic treatment, encompassing body, mind and soul of the patient. Apart from conventional medication-based pain therapy, there is also a variety of non-medicinal treatments for pain. One of these methods is hypnosis, an imaginative treatment that activates available resources; it is not only an effective way of alleviating pain, but it also can ease psychological problems at the same time.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
